import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path('/workspace/sites/olympiad-atlas'); new=[]
def add(year,num,rating,topic,tags,statement,reason,shared=None,comp='USAJMO',solution=None):
 url=f'https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/{"JMO" if comp=="USAJMO" else "USAMO"}-{year}-notes.pdf'
 apps=[dict(competition=comp,year=year,number=str(num))]
 if shared:apps.append(dict(competition='USAMO',year=year,number=str(shared)))
 new.append(dict(id=f'us-{comp.lower()}-{year}-{num}',country='United States',competition=comp,year=year,number=str(num),statement=statement,solution=solution or f'[View Evan Chen’s worked solution notes]({url}).',sourceUrl=url,solutionUrl=url,solutionKind='original-exposition' if solution else 'source-link',topic=topic,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=rating,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Statement restated in original wording; mathematical argument individually checked. Linked notes are author-maintained, not labelled official.',appearances=apps))
add(2019,1,5,'Combinatorics','Parity|Invariants|Inductive constructions',r'Let $a,b$ be positive integers. Number $a+b$ bowls from left to right. Initially bowls $1,\ldots,a$ each hold one apple, and the remaining bowls each hold one pear. In one move, move an apple from $i$ to $i+1$ and a pear from $j$ to $j-1$, requiring $i-j$ to be even. Bowls may hold several fruits. Prove that one can finish with a pear in each of the first $b$ bowls and an apple in each of the last $a$ bowls exactly when $ab$ is even.','The difference between odd-positioned apple and pear counts is invariant, giving the obstruction. Sufficiency requires an induction with different outer-fruit pairings depending on the parity of a+b; spotting the obstruction alone does not finish the problem.')
add(2019,2,5.5,'Algebra','Functional equations|Bijections|Pigeonhole principle',r'Determine every integer pair $(a,b)$ for which functions $f,g:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$ can satisfy $f(g(x))=x+a$ and $g(f(x))=x+b$ for every integer $x$.','Both functions are bijections, and composing the identities gives f(x+b)=f(x)+a. Comparing residue classes using injectivity and surjectivity forces equal absolute shifts; constructing both signs then completes the answer.')
add(2019,3,7,'Geometry','Cyclic quadrilaterals|Symmedians|Similarity',r'The diagonals of cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$ meet at $E$, and $AD^2+BC^2=AB^2$. Point $P$ lies on side $AB$ with $\angle APD=\angle BPC$. Show that $PE$ passes through the midpoint of $CD$.','The length condition must be converted into a squared ratio on AB. Similar triangles identify the special P, and a symmedian-to-median correspondence through the intersecting diagonals gives the midpoint. Several non-obvious geometric links place this at the upper edge.',shared=2)
add(2019,4,5.5,'Geometry','Excircles|Similarity|Tangency',r'In triangle $ABC$ with $\angle ABC>90^\circ$, let $E,F$ be the perpendicular feet from $B,C$ onto the opposite sidelines. Is it possible for line $EF$ to be tangent to the $A$-excircle? Prove your answer.','The orthic triangle AEF is similar to ABC. If the same excircle were tangent to EF, equal exradii would force equal similarity scale and an impossible rectangular configuration. Recognising the second triangle and checking the obtuse configuration are the main steps.')
add(2019,5,5.5,'Combinatorics','Counting|Partially ordered sets|Bijections',r'For a nonnegative integer $n$, count arrays of sets $(S_{ij})_{0\le i,j\le n}$ drawn from $\{1,\ldots,2n\}$ such that $|S_{ij}|=i+j$ and $S_{ij}\subseteq S_{kl}$ whenever $i\le k$ and $j\le l$.','A boundary chain determines a permutation. Fill the remaining cells in diagonal order: each missing intermediate set has precisely two choices. The main work is selecting an order that makes these independent binary choices rigorous.',shared=4)
add(2019,6,6,'Number theory','Modular invariants|Rational numbers|Dyadic averaging',r'For coprime positive integers $m,n$, start with $m/n$ and $n/m$ written on a board. Repeatedly choose two existing numbers $x,y$ and append either $(x+y)/2$ or $2xy/(x+y)$. Determine exactly which $(m,n)$ allow $1$ to appear after finitely many operations.','An odd prime divisor of m+n makes every obtainable value congruent to −1, with denominators controlled. For the complementary case, an explicit weighted average with power-of-two denominator is built by repeated ordinary averaging. Both the modular obstruction and constructive direction are essential.',shared=5)
add(2018,2,4.5,'Algebra','Inequalities|Homogeneity|Substitution|Factorisation',r'Positive reals $a,b,c$ satisfy $a+b+c=4\sqrt[3]{abc}$. Prove $2(ab+bc+ca)+4\min(a^2,b^2,c^2)\ge a^2+b^2+c^2$.','Normalize the smallest variable to 1, then use the condition to eliminate the sum of the other two. A cube-root substitution reduces the claim to a single factored nonnegative cubic; the choice of normalization is the key step.',shared=1)
add(2018,3,7,'Geometry','Simson line|Orthocentres|Reflections|Cyclic quadrilaterals',r'Quadrilateral $ABCD$ lies on circle $\omega$, with $AC\perp BD$. Reflect $D$ in lines $BA,BC$ to obtain $E,F$, and put $P=BD\cap EF$. Circles $EPD,FPD$ meet $\omega$ again at $Q,R$, respectively, each different from $D$. Prove $EQ=FR$.','A Simson-line construction identifies P as the orthocentre of ABC. One must then recognise Q and R as reflected orthocentres and use isosceles trapezoids. The task is principally to discover these hidden point identities rather than execute one angle chase.')
add(2018,4,6,'Algebra','Geometry inequalities|Sum of squares|Equality cases',r'Find every real $x$ for which some triangle $ABC$ has circumradius $2$, angle $ABC\ge90^\circ$, and $x^4+ax^3+bx^2+cx+1=0$, where $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$.','The geometry gives a squared-side bound and b≤4. A carefully chosen sum of squares turns the polynomial into a nonnegative expression; simultaneous equality constraints then determine the two possible roots and verify realizability.')
add(2018,5,5.5,'Combinatorics','Averaging|Modular arithmetic|Collision counting',r'Let $p$ be prime and $a_1,\ldots,a_p$ integers. Show that for some integer $k$, the numbers $a_1+k,a_2+2k,\ldots,a_p+pk$ occupy at least $p/2$ different residue classes modulo $p$.','Every pair of indices collides for exactly one k modulo p. Averaging the collision count gives a good k; a second count relates collisions to the number of occupied classes. Choosing collisions as the quantity to average is the principal insight.',shared=4)
add(2018,6,6.5,'Combinatorics','Permutations|Inversions|Relabelling invariants',r'Arrange cards $1,\ldots,n$ in any order. For $i=1,2,\ldots,n$ in turn, remove card $i$ and reinsert it so that the number of cards now to its right equals the number formerly to its left. Prove that the final order has the same number of inversions as the initial order. An inversion is a pair of cards in decreasing order of labels.','The decisive trick is to replace each moved label i by n+i. It changes from the smallest remaining label to the largest, so reversing its position preserves its inversion contribution at every step. Finding this artificial relabelling invariant is substantially harder than checking it.')
add(2019,1,6.5,'Algebra','Functional equations|Iteration|Orbit minima|Injectivity',r'A function $f:\mathbb Z_{>0}\to\mathbb Z_{>0}$ satisfies $f^{\circ f(n)}(n)=n^2/f(f(n))$ for every positive integer $n$, where $f^{\circ k}$ means $k$ successive applications of $f$. Find all possible values of $f(1000)$.','Injectivity follows from comparing equal outputs. Taking the least integer in an iteration orbit forces a two-cycle, and injectivity propagates this back along the orbit. Parity then classifies the allowed cycles. The variable iteration count disguises this useful orbit-minimum argument.',comp='USAMO')
add(2021,3,7,'Geometry','Packing|Area bounds|Convexity|Difference bodies',r'An equilateral triangle of side length $L>0$ contains $n$ equilateral triangles of side length $1$ with pairwise disjoint interiors. The small triangles have sides parallel to those of the large triangle and point in the opposite direction. Prove $n\le\frac23L^2$.','The triangles can be replaced by larger, non-overlapping regular hexagons with the same centres. Proving both the non-overlap and containment of this enlargement is the substantial insight; the area comparison is then immediate.',solution=r'''Place the centroid of a unit small triangle at the origin and call its closed region $T$. Define $T-T=\{u-v:u,v\in T\}$ and $H=\tfrac12(T-T)$. The region $H$ is a regular hexagon of side $1/2$, so its area is $3\sqrt3/8$.

Two translates $K+u,K+v$ of a convex region have overlapping interiors exactly when $u-v$ is in the interior of $K-K$: this follows by subtracting one interior point from another. Since $T-T$ is centrally symmetric and convex, $H-H=T-T$. Consequently, replacing each small triangle $T+c$ by $H+c$ preserves the condition that interiors do not overlap.

These hexagons also remain in the large triangle. In each outward normal direction of a side of the large triangle, the opposite orientation means that $T$ has a vertex at distance $1/\sqrt3$ from its centroid in that direction. The farthest point of $H$ in that direction has projection $\sqrt3/4$, which is smaller. Thus each of the three bounding half-planes that contains $T+c$ also contains $H+c$.

Adding the areas of the disjoint hexagons gives $n(3\sqrt3/8)\le\sqrt3 L^2/4$, or $n\le2L^2/3$.''')
data=json.loads((p/'data/americas.json').read_text()); ids={x['id'] for x in new}; data=[x for x in data if x['id'] not in ids]+new
(p/'data/americas.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
c=json.loads((p/'research/americas-coverage.json').read_text())
full={2019:[5,5.5,7,5.5,5.5,6],2018:[3.5,4.5,7,6,5.5,6.5],2021:[5.5,5.5,7,6,7,5.5]}
c['fullyScreened']=[x for x in c['fullyScreened'] if not(x['competition']=='USAJMO' and x['year'] in full)]
c['partiallyScreened']=[x for x in c['partiallyScreened'] if not(x['competition']=='USAJMO' and x['year'] in full)]
for year,ratings in full.items():c['fullyScreened'].append(dict(competition='USAJMO',year=year,ratings=ratings,source=f'https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/JMO-{year}-notes.pdf'))
for year,ratings in {2019:{'1':6.5,'2':7,'4':5.5,'5':6},2018:{'1':4.5,'4':5.5}}.items():
 old=next((x for x in c['partiallyScreened'] if (x['competition'],x['year'])==('USAMO',year)),None)
 if old:old['ratings'].update(ratings)
 else:c['partiallyScreened'].append(dict(competition='USAMO',year=year,ratings=ratings,pending=[str(i) for i in range(1,7) if str(i) not in ratings]))
c['papersScreened']=len(c['fullyScreened']);c['problemAppearancesScreened']=sum(len(x['ratings']) for x in c['fullyScreened']+c['partiallyScreened']);c['uniqueIncluded']=len(data)
c['gaps'][1]='US: USAJMO 2018–2026 fully screened; earlier USAJMO years pending except listed entries. USAMO 2006–2014 screening and recent selected/shared problems are listed individually; outstanding problems and other years remain pending.'
(p/'research/americas-coverage.json').write_text(json.dumps(c,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Total',len(data))
